# ASan guard for the element-borrow use-after-free class.
#
# Three shapes that all held a borrow into a `Vector[String]`'s buffer across
# a reallocating growth, and all read freed memory. stdout is a weak
# instrument for this class -- freed memory frequently still holds the right
# bytes -- so the sanitizer is what actually adjudicates it here. Each shape
# is ALSO wired as a stdout regression fixture at the top level; this file is
# the memory-validity instrument, not a duplicate of that role.
#
# 1. A view spelled through a SECOND NAME for the collection (`alias.get`).
#    The recorded provenance named the spelling instead of the storage owner,
#    so the rescue was never emitted.
# 2. The SAME element spelled through the collection's own name, live at the
#    same time -- the invariance half.
# 3. An element read with `v[i]`, no alias anywhere. Here the rescue side was
#    the defect: the anonymous index-load temp stayed tagged as a borrow into
#    the collection, so the severance walk cloned it at every mutation, FROM a
#    pointer the previous iteration's realloc had already freed.
#
# Baseline: shapes 1-3 each rc 139 SIGSEGV, and under `--sanitize`
# heap-use-after-free in `gorget_string_copy_cow` / `gorget_string_clone_to_owned`.
# ⚠ The realloc is load-bearing (64 pushes). The payload's heap-ness is NOT --
# a literal reproduces it just as well.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    Vector[String] v = []
    v.push(mk("hel", "lo"))
    Vector[String] alias = v
    String spelled = alias.get(0).unwrap()
    String direct = v.get(0).unwrap()
    String indexed = v[0]
    String indexed_alias = alias[0]
    int i = 0
    while i < 64:
        v.push(mk("fill", "er"))
        i = i + 1
    print(spelled)
    print(direct)
    print(indexed)
    print(indexed_alias)
